WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.111
Pope Francis has developed a reputation for
being a bit...

00:00:03.111 --> 00:00:04.709
Unconventional.

00:00:04.709 --> 00:00:07.365
Since his papacy began in 2013,

00:00:07.365 --> 00:00:09.136
he’s done away with ring kissing.

00:00:09.136 --> 00:00:10.620
"Because he wants to be humble."

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:11.620
Throne sitting.

00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:14.002
"Pope Francis used the simple white chair."

00:00:14.002 --> 00:00:14.928
Pope mobiling.

00:00:14.928 --> 00:00:17.392
"Refusing to use his bulletproof vehicles."

00:00:17.392 --> 00:00:20.470
He’s broken a ton of centuries-old traditions.

00:00:20.470 --> 00:00:24.000
Pushing for modern updates across 
the Catholic church.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:28.422
But it’s not just what Pope Francis does
that sets him apart from past popes.

00:00:28.422 --> 00:00:30.229
It’s what he wears.

00:00:30.229 --> 00:00:33.642
From elaborate embroidered scenes 
to more subtle color cues.

00:00:33.642 --> 00:00:36.762
Papal clothing has always 
been laden with symbolism.

00:00:36.762 --> 00:00:42.066
And it’s is how Pope Francis communicates
some of his most basic beliefs and intentions.

00:00:46.819 --> 00:00:49.851
Most of the time, Pope Francis wears this.

00:00:49.851 --> 00:00:51.220
When greeting crowds,

00:00:51.220 --> 00:00:52.860
meeting dignitaries,

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:56.299
Basically anything other than performing mass.

00:00:56.299 --> 00:00:59.469
It’s made up of a cassock,
an ankle length coat.

00:00:59.469 --> 00:01:01.807
A fascia, tied around his waist.

00:01:01.807 --> 00:01:05.898
A zucchetto, which translates to “skullcap”
for obvious reasons.

00:01:05.898 --> 00:01:09.194
And this, a pellegrina: 
a cape of sorts.

00:01:09.194 --> 00:01:12.457
Which, unfortunately, often blows in the wind

00:01:12.457 --> 00:01:16.776
prompting an ongoing heated debate 
over whether or not to add velcro.

00:01:16.776 --> 00:01:18.270
Anyway...

00:01:18.270 --> 00:01:21.940
The pope’s clothing is roughly the same
as a cardinal’s below him,

00:01:21.940 --> 00:01:23.739
or bishops below them,

00:01:23.739 --> 00:01:27.709
Except, all of the pope’s clothing is, 
of course, white.

00:01:27.709 --> 00:01:30.687
The biblical color of angels 
and Jesus’ robes.

00:01:30.687 --> 00:01:33.838
Symbolizing innocence, purity, divinity.

00:01:33.838 --> 00:01:37.666
This outfit has been roughly the same 
since at least the 1200s.

00:01:37.666 --> 00:01:42.755
And since the late 1700s, it’s even been
made by the same family of tailors.

00:01:42.755 --> 00:01:46.344
But each pope adds their own specific flair.

00:01:46.344 --> 00:01:49.397
Compare Pope Francis to Pope John Paul II.

00:01:49.397 --> 00:01:51.184
He was two Popes ago.

00:01:51.184 --> 00:01:52.982
We couldn't find a Pope Benedict doll,

00:01:52.982 --> 00:01:55.367
probably because he wasn't pope 
for very long.

00:01:55.367 --> 00:01:58.595
Even in doll form, the differences 
are easy to see.

00:01:58.595 --> 00:02:00.467
Pope Francis’ fascia is plain,

00:02:00.467 --> 00:02:03.349
instead of being fringed and 
bearing his coat of arms.

00:02:03.349 --> 00:02:06.649
And his crucifix is iron, as opposed to gold.

00:02:06.649 --> 00:02:09.866
Francis also keeps it plain 
when it comes to outerwear,

00:02:09.866 --> 00:02:13.870
opting for a white peacoat, instead of the
traditional winter mozzetta

00:02:13.870 --> 00:02:15.629
lined with ermine fur.

00:02:15.629 --> 00:02:19.578
That’s this extremely cute weasel-like animal.

00:02:19.578 --> 00:02:21.520
And then there’s the shoes.

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:24.850
Popes usually pick one style of shoes 
and stick to them.

00:02:24.850 --> 00:02:29.099
Pope Francis wears frugal, black oxfords,
paired with black socks.

00:02:29.099 --> 00:02:32.694
The same shoes he wore 
as a priest and a cardinal.

00:02:32.694 --> 00:02:37.244
But before him, most popes opted for 
white socks and red shoes.

00:02:37.244 --> 00:02:39.581
JP II wore a dark, burgundy pair.

00:02:39.581 --> 00:02:43.790
And Pope Benedict famously sported 
slick red leather loafers

00:02:43.790 --> 00:02:50.042
which prompted Esquire Magazine to name him
“accessessorizer of the year” in 2007.

00:02:50.042 --> 00:02:53.368
The red shoes are likely a reference 
to the blood of martyrs

00:02:53.368 --> 00:02:58.424
and the passion of Jesus, who is often pictured
in red post-resurrection.

00:02:58.424 --> 00:03:00.930
But some claim they’re just 
a holdover from when

00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:03.168
the rich and powerful wore a lot of red.

00:03:03.168 --> 00:03:06.830
Because it was expensive and 
set them apart from common people.

00:03:06.830 --> 00:03:11.670
Which it seems is where a lot of 
papal garment design originates.

00:03:11.670 --> 00:03:13.989
If you look at the clothing of Byzantine royalty

00:03:13.989 --> 00:03:18.290
you’ll see a lot of similarities 
to modern catholic leadership.

00:03:18.290 --> 00:03:21.230
Right down to the red shoes.

00:03:21.230 --> 00:03:25.727
Pope Francis’ choices reflect a desire to
distance himself from that history.

00:03:31.180 --> 00:03:34.909
He even wears some of the same mass vestments
he did as bishop.

00:03:34.909 --> 00:03:39.000
Including, this, his mitre.

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:42.260
Mitres, essentially foldable hats, 
have been around

00:03:42.260 --> 00:03:45.499
in various iterations for at least 
a thousand years.

00:03:45.499 --> 00:03:48.329
Today, they’re worn only during mass.

00:03:48.329 --> 00:03:52.109
Not just by the pope, but by cardinals 
and bishops too.

00:03:52.109 --> 00:03:54.680
No one can really agree on their origin

00:03:54.680 --> 00:03:58.099
but they’re likely yet another holdover
from royal dress.

00:03:58.099 --> 00:04:01.828
There are a few mass vestments
reserved only for the pope.

00:04:01.828 --> 00:04:04.966
Including the pallium, 
this circular wool band.

00:04:04.966 --> 00:04:08.150
And sometimes, this thing: a ferula.

00:04:08.150 --> 00:04:11.737
Both references to Jesus as “the good shepherd”.

00:04:11.737 --> 00:04:15.905
But the main event of the pope's mass attire
is the chasuble.

00:04:15.905 --> 00:04:18.354
Essentially a poncho.

00:04:18.354 --> 00:04:21.647
This chasuble, white with a 
minimal gold trim

00:04:21.647 --> 00:04:24.226
is the first one Pope Francis wore.

00:04:24.226 --> 00:04:26.622
His choice for his inaugural mass.

00:04:26.622 --> 00:04:30.982
Traditionally, the time for your 
showiest chasuble.

00:04:32.396 --> 00:04:36.880
It’s also his chasuble of choice, 
worn most frequently throughout the year.

00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:42.551
Even on Christmas, another time popes 
usually opt for more opulence.

00:04:42.551 --> 00:04:47.310
But over the course of the year, chasuble
colors vary according to the church calendar

00:04:47.310 --> 00:04:50.264
a schedule all priests roughly follow.

00:04:50.264 --> 00:04:54.000
Purple, during times of penance: like advent
and lent.

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:58.474
Green, during times of growth: used throughout
quote “ordinary time”.

00:04:58.474 --> 00:05:02.634
White and gold, symbolizing joy and purity:
for major celebrations like

00:05:02.634 --> 00:05:04.289
Easter and Christmas.

00:05:04.289 --> 00:05:08.450
Sprinkled in are some Red chasubles: 
whenever sacrifice is celebrated.

00:05:08.450 --> 00:05:11.410
And a few pink ones.

00:05:11.410 --> 00:05:13.545
Pope Francis usually follows this calendar.

00:05:13.545 --> 00:05:19.090
But on July 8th, 2013 he wore this.

00:05:19.090 --> 00:05:21.526
A simple, purple chasuble.

00:05:21.526 --> 00:05:25.280
Even though it was squarely within 
Ordinary time.

00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:28.750
Purple, the color of penance 
and mourning.

00:05:28.750 --> 00:05:33.710
He wore it during a visit to a refugee camp
on the Italian island of Lampedusa.

00:05:33.710 --> 00:05:38.091
While standing atop an altar made of 
wooden remnants from refugee boats.

00:05:38.091 --> 00:05:42.960
He delivered a now famous homily that criticized
global indifference to the refugee crisis.

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:57.081
His clothes represent the church 
he’s working to build:

00:05:57.081 --> 00:06:00.718
something simpler, more accessible, 
more modern.

